Subject: [PATCH 0/2] Bluetooth: btusb: Pick the SCO alternate setting from the links that are up

btusb chooses the isochronous alternate setting from the last SCO
notification, not from the SCO links that exist. When one of two SCO
links goes down, the remaining link is moved to alternate setting 0,
which has no isochronous bandwidth. A SCO connection is also counted
from the start of its setup: if another connection is added or removed
while it is being set up, the notification of the link coming up is
ignored.

 1: keep the air mode of a SCO link that came up in the connection and
    add a helper that counts the links that are up by air mode, both
    counts from one walk of the connection list under hdev->lock.
 2: let btusb_work() select the alternate setting from those counts.
    It takes hdev->lock only for the count and releases it before any
    USB power management or set-interface call.

Patch 2 needs patch 1.

Changes since the first draft: the air mode is stored before the
connection is marked connected; one helper takes both counts in one
pass under hdev->lock (lockdep_assert_held()) instead of two separate
walks under RCU, which could see no CVSD link, then a CVSD link come up
and the transparent one go down, and so select alternate setting 0
with a link up.

Tested with a userspace controller attached over usbip to a QEMU guest
(the real btusb and core; KASAN, lockdep, four CPUs): five scenarios
(two mSBC links with either one hung up first, two CVSD links, and a
single link set up while another connection is added and removed, for
mSBC and for CVSD). Unpatched, the setting is wrong at some point in
all five; with the series all five pass, in two runs each (0 of 10,
then 10 of 10). A sixth case changes the connection set while the work
is between its looks at the links (two mSBC links; one hangs up, which
starts the work; while it is running a CVSD link comes up and the other
mSBC link hangs up; the work is held in a test-kernel delay). With two
separate counts it selects alternate setting 0 with a CVSD link up in
3 of 3 runs; with the one-pass count in none of 3. No lockdep report
in any run.

With a CVSD and a transparent link up at the same time the setting is
the transparent one, as the patched btusb code chooses it; whether that
carries the CVSD link is a question for the review (Core Volume 4,
Part B, Table 2.1: alternate setting 1 is 9 bytes per frame, one 8-bit
voice channel).

The series applies on bluetooth-next without conflicts.
The existing sco-tester results are the same with and without it.

